# RansomGuard IR Backend — Environment Configuration

Copy to `.env` (or set as system environment variables) — all optional; defaults
work for local development. No secrets are required.

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `HOST` | `0.0.0.0` | Bind address |
| `PORT` | `8000` | Bind port (honored by `python app.main:app`; pass `--port` for uvicorn CLI) |
| `CORS_ORIGINS` | `*` | Comma-separated allowed origins, e.g. `https://myapp.github.io,http://localhost:5173` |
| `RANSOMGUARD_DB_PATH` | `backend/ransomguard.db` | SQLite database file location |
| `RANSOMGUARD_EVIDENCE_DIR` | `backend/evidence_storage/` | Forensic artifact storage |
| `DETECTION_COOLDOWN_SECONDS` | `300` | Per-host duplicate-detection cooldown |

## Production startup

```bash
cd backend
pip install -r requirements.txt

# option A (recommended, explicit):
python -m uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000

# option B (honors $HOST/$PORT from the environment, e.g. PaaS providers):
python -m app.main
```

`python -m app.main` (option C) runs uvicorn programmatically with `HOST`/`PORT`
from the environment — this is what most free hosts (Render, Railway, Fly, Koyeb)
expect: set the start command to `python -m app.main` and they inject `PORT`.

## Notes

- SQLite is file-based — the demo needs no database server. For persistence on a
  PaaS with an ephemeral filesystem, set `RANSOMGUARD_DB_PATH` to a mounted path.
- Keep `CORS_ORIGINS` restricted in any shared deployment (e.g. your GitHub Pages
  origin) instead of `*`.
- The detector agents (VM + host) connect to `http://<backend-ip>:8000` — make
  sure the port is reachable on your network/firewall.
